      "Ans": "Despite multiple attempts to adjust exposure times (ranging from 30ms to 100ms) and moving the stage to various positions around the center, I am unable to visually identify any graphene piece or distinct features on the substrate; the images remain uniformly dark or featureless.\n\nBecause graphene is often nearly transparent on a silicon substrate, it can be extremely difficult to locate using only optical microscopy. To resolve this, I propose switching from visual search to **spectral mapping**. By performing a grid scan over the central area and analyzing the resulting Raman spectra, I can pinpoint the exact location of the graphene by detecting its characteristic G and 2D bands.\n\n**Proposed Plan:**\n1. Define a small grid (e.g., 5x5) centered around the middle of the sample holder (~37.8 mm, 25.2 mm).\n2. Use the recommended parameters for graphene: **20% laser power** and **2s exposure**.\n3. Map the area and identify the point with the strongest graphene signal.\n4. Perform a high-quality measurement at that specific location.\n\nWould you like me to proceed with this grid scan, or do you have a known coordinate where I should focus my search?",
